        <div id="protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Has More Protein per Scoop, Naked or Optimum Nutrition?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Naked Whey has slightly more protein per serving, 25 g against 24 g, and noticeably more per gram of powder. Per the labels, about 83% of a Naked Whey serving is protein, against about 77% of a Gold Standard scoop. That six-point gap is the space taken up in Gold Standard by cocoa, lecithin, flavoring and sweetener, which Naked's unflavored bag simply does not contain.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice the difference is small. To reach 25 g of protein you need about 30 g of Naked Whey or about 32 g of Gold Standard, and both land at roughly 120 to 125 calories for that 25 g. Neither will move your daily total in a way you would notice. If you count every gram, Naked is the more efficient powder; if you do not, call this round a tie.
          </p>

          <p>
            What matters more is the serving itself. Naked's 30 g serving is "about 2 scoops," per its label, while Gold Standard's 31 g is one scoop. That makes Gold Standard faster to measure and less messy for a gym bag, and it is one reason buyers who switch from ON to Naked sometimes think they are getting less protein than they are. Read the serving size, not the scoop count.
          </p>

          <p>
            On amino acids, Naked publishes 2.98 g of leucine per serving, comfortably above the roughly 2 to 3 g that research on muscle protein synthesis treats as a useful dose per meal. Gold Standard states 5.5 g of total BCAAs but no leucine figure. Both are whey, so leucine will be in the same general range, but only Naked lets you check it on the label. For more on how much protein you need in a day, see our <a href='/how-much-protein-do-i-need/'>protein calculator guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Naked vs Optimum Nutrition: Which Is Cheaper per Gram of Protein?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gold Standard is cheaper, at about $1.44 per 25 g of protein against about $1.84 for Naked Whey, so Naked costs roughly 28% more per gram. Those figures use the prices in our database: $79.99 for a 58-serving, 3.96 lb tub of Gold Standard at Target, and $139.99 for a 76-serving, 5 lb bag of Naked Whey on Naked's store, both checked on 1 November 2026.
          </p>

