          <p>
            At one 25 g serving a day, that gap is about $12 a month: roughly $43 for Gold Standard against $55 for Naked Whey. It is real money over a year (about $145), but it is smaller than the gap between Gold Standard and most premium "clean label" brands, and Naked's per-gram price is still lower than many grass-fed competitors in our database.
          </p>

          <p>
            Container size changes the answer. Optimum Nutrition's own store listed the 1.98 lb Double Rich Chocolate tub at $74.49 for 29 servings when our database was checked, which works out to about $2.68 per 25 g, far more than Naked's 5 lb bag. Gold Standard is cheap only in the large tubs and at big-box retailers. Naked's smaller bags cost more per gram too; we did not record their serving counts, but going by weight alone the 2 lb bag at $62.99 should land somewhere above $2 per 25 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands go on sale often, and Gold Standard in particular is sold by a long list of supplement retailers and big-box stores, so the price you see will move. The method stays the same: divide the price by the number of servings, then scale to 25 g of protein. Our <a href='/best-cheap-protein-powder/'>cheapest protein powder rankings</a> run that math across every powder we track.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Is Third-Party Tested, Naked or Optimum Nutrition?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gold Standard has the certification you can check: Double Rich Chocolate and Vanilla Ice Cream appear in the NSF Certified for Sport database, which we confirmed on 19 November 2026. NSF Certified for Sport screens finished product for substances banned in sport and checks that the label matches what is in the tub. Other Gold Standard flavors were not listed when we checked, so if you are drug tested, buy one of those two.
          </p>

          <p>
            Naked says Naked Whey is NSF Contents Certified, and the badge appears on its own label image. Contents Certified checks label accuracy and screens for contaminants; it is not a sport certification and does not test for banned substances. We could not confirm the claim for Naked Whey: when our database last checked NSF's public listing for Naked's parent company, it showed only Naked Energy and Naked Creatine. That may reflect how NSF lists products rather than a lapsed certification, but until it shows up in the registry we treat it as a brand claim.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">IF YOU ARE DRUG TESTED</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy Gold Standard in Double Rich Chocolate or Vanilla Ice Cream, and check the NSF listing first.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Neither Naked Whey nor the other Gold Standard flavors carried a sport certification we could verify. Certification is granted product by product and flavor by flavor, so search the <a href='https://www.nsfsport.com/certified-products/' rel='nofollow noopener' target='_blank'>NSF Certified for Sport database</a> for the exact tub before you buy. Our <a href='/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/'>NSF certified protein powder guide</a> lists other options.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            For readers who are not tested athletes, the question is really about contaminants such as heavy metals. Neither brand publishes a lab report for Naked Whey or Gold Standard on its product page, and our database has no heavy-metal results for either. Whey generally tests lower for heavy metals than plant proteins, but "generally" is not a result. Our guide to <a href='/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/'>protein powder without heavy metals</a> lists brands that do publish numbers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Which Has the Cleaner Label?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Naked Whey has the cleaner label by any definition: its ingredient list is one item, whey protein concentrate. Gold Standard Double Rich Chocolate lists a protein blend (whey isolate, whey concentrate and hydrolyzed whey), alkalized cocoa, sunflower and/or soy lecithin, natural and artificial flavor, and acesulfame potassium.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of Gold Standard's extra ingredients is a safety concern at the amounts used; acesulfame potassium is approved by the FDA, and lecithin is there to help the powder dissolve. But plenty of buyers avoid artificial sweeteners on principle or because they notice an aftertaste, and anyone with a soy allergy has to treat "sunflower and/or soy lecithin" as soy. Naked Whey avoids both questions entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Naked's sourcing claims go further: it says the whey comes from grass-fed cows on US farms, not treated with rBGH or rBST, and that the product is non-GMO. Those are brand claims, not third-party certifications, and grass-fed whey has not been shown to build more muscle than conventional whey. It matters if you care about how the dairy was produced; it does not change the nutrition panel. Our <a href='/best-grass-fed-whey-protein/'>best grass-fed whey guide</a> compares Naked with other grass-fed brands.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a fair point on Gold Standard's side, too. A label with more ingredients is not automatically worse, and Gold Standard's blend includes whey isolate, which carries less lactose and fat than concentrate. If "clean" to you means low sugar, Gold Standard's 1 g beats Naked's 2 g. If it means nothing added, Naked wins easily. If you want flavor without artificial sweeteners, see our list of <a href='/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/'>protein powders without artificial sweeteners</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Tastes Better and Mixes Easier?
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are not really competing on taste, because unflavored Naked Whey is not meant to taste of anything but milk. We have not tasted either powder; what follows is what buyers commonly report in retailer reviews. Gold Standard is widely described as easy to drink with water and as mixing with a shaker bottle in a few seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buyers describe unflavored Naked Whey as mild, slightly milky and close to neutral in a smoothie, and less pleasant mixed with plain water. Because it contains no lecithin, some buyers report that it foams or clumps more in a shaker than ON does, and that a blender fixes it. That matches what you would expect from a powder with nothing added to help it dissolve.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flavor range is lopsided. Optimum Nutrition lists about 24 Gold Standard flavors, from Double Rich Chocolate and Vanilla Ice Cream to Mocha Cappuccino and Cookies & Cream. Naked Whey comes unflavored, with separate Chocolate, Vanilla, Strawberry and Chocolate Peanut Butter versions that add flavoring and sweetener and have their own labels. If you want variety in a ready-to-shake powder, Gold Standard is the easier choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where unflavored Naked earns its place is in recipes. It does not fight the flavor of fruit, coffee, oats or pancake batter, and it does not add sweetness to savory food. If most of your protein goes into a blender or an oven rather than a shaker, see our <a href='/best-unflavored-protein-powder/'>best unflavored protein powder</a> and <a href='/best-protein-powder-for-baking/'>baking</a> guides.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stomach" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Is Easier on the Stomach?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gold Standard is likely to be slightly easier for people sensitive to lactose, because its protein blend lists whey isolate first, and isolate is filtered to remove most of the lactose. Naked Whey is 100% concentrate, which keeps more of it. Neither label states a lactose figure, so this is an inference from the protein types and the sugar line: 1 g of sugar per Gold Standard scoop against 2 g in Naked Whey.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people, neither will cause trouble. A few grams of lactose is well below what many lactose-intolerant adults tolerate in a sitting. But if milk reliably upsets your stomach, both of these powders contain concentrate, and neither adds lactase or digestive enzymes, so a pure isolate is the safer choice. Our <a href='/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/'>lactose-free protein powder</a> guide covers the options.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other variable is the additives. A minority of people report bloating or loose stools from sweeteners and gums, and Gold Standard contains acesulfame potassium, while Naked Whey contains nothing but whey. If you have had trouble with flavored powders in the past and suspect the sweetener rather than the dairy, unflavored Naked is the cleaner test.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have a diagnosed digestive condition, kidney disease, or a milk allergy rather than an intolerance, talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before choosing either. A milk allergy rules out both: both are made from cow's milk.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="naked-pros-cons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. What Naked Whey Does Well and Where It Falls Short
          </h2>

          <p>
            Naked Whey does one thing very well: it gives you about 25 g of protein with nothing else attached. One ingredient, no sweetener, no flavoring, no gums, no lecithin, grass-fed dairy per the brand, and a published amino acid profile with 2.98 g of leucine. At about 83% protein by weight it is one of the denser concentrates in our database, and its Label Score of 74 reflects that.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where it falls short</h3>

          <p>
            The weak spots are price, certification and convenience. At about $1.84 per 25 g it costs more than Gold Standard's big tub, and much more if you buy small bags. The NSF Contents Certified claim is on the label but was not in NSF's public listing when we checked, and it is not a sport certification in any case. And a 30 g serving that takes about two scoops of a powder that tastes of milk is not what most people want from a post-workout shake.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is also worth knowing what you do not get. Naked Whey has no added enzymes, so it is not the best choice for sensitive stomachs, and the flavored versions are separate products whose labels differ. If you like Naked but want lower lactose, the brand's whey isolate is the better fit; our <a href='/naked-nutrition-protein-powder-review/'>Naked review</a> covers it, though it is not yet in our label database, so we have not compared its numbers here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="on-pros-cons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. What Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard Does Well and Where It Falls Short
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gold Standard does the basics at a price few brands match: 24 g of protein in one scoop, about $1.44 per 25 g in the large tub, about two dozen flavors, and NSF Certified for Sport on its two best-selling flavors. It is sold almost everywhere, from Target and Amazon to most supplement shops, so it is easy to restock. That combination is why its Label Score, 78, is the higher of the two.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where it falls short</h3>

          <p>
            The label is the main compromise. Acesulfame potassium, artificial flavor and sunflower and/or soy lecithin are all on the Double Rich Chocolate panel, so it fails the test for anyone avoiding artificial sweeteners or soy. ON does not publish leucine, and grocery listings show more than one version of the panel, so the exact numbers depend on the tub. The sport certification covers only two flavors, which is easy to miss.
          </p>

          <p>
            Price also depends heavily on size. The 1.98 lb tub on ON's own store works out to about $2.68 per 25 g, more than Naked's 5 lb bag. If you are comparing Gold Standard with other mainstream brands, our <a href='/ghost-vs-optimum-nutrition/'>Ghost vs Optimum Nutrition</a> and <a href='/isopure-vs-optimum-nutrition/'>Isopure vs Optimum Nutrition</a> comparisons run the same math against other flavored wheys.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. Which Should You Buy, Naked or Optimum Nutrition?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Gold Standard if you mostly drink protein as a shake with water or milk and want it to taste like something. It is cheaper per gram in the big tub, it mixes in one scoop, and if you compete in a tested sport it is the only one of the two with a certification you can verify. Buy Double Rich Chocolate or Vanilla Ice Cream if testing matters; buy any flavor you like if it does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Naked Whey if you blend protein into smoothies, oatmeal, yogurt or baking, or if you read the ingredient list before the nutrition panel. Paying about 40 cents more per 25 g buys you a powder with nothing in it but grass-fed whey, which is the whole point of the brand. It is also the better choice if you avoid soy or artificial sweeteners, or want to control the sweetness yourself.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you cannot decide, think about where the powder ends up. People who make shakes tend to be happier with Gold Standard; people who cook with protein tend to be happier with Naked. Some buyers keep one of each. For the full field beyond these two, our <a href='/best-whey-protein-powder/'>best whey protein powder</a> rankings compare every whey we track on Label Score and cost per 25 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands also sell powders beyond the flagship. If you want a slow-digesting protein before bed, <a href='/protein-powder/naked-casein/'>Naked Casein</a> scores 86 in our database at about $1.39 per 25 g, against 63 for <a href='/protein-powder/optimum-nutrition-gold-standard-casein/'>Gold Standard Casein</a> at about $1.70, per the labels and prices we recorded. That is a different comparison from this one, but it is the one place where Naked wins on price as well as label.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip-both" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            14. Who Should Skip Both?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip both if you are lactose intolerant enough that ordinary whey gives you trouble. Naked Whey is all concentrate and Gold Standard contains concentrate, and neither adds lactase. A whey isolate, or a <a href='/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/'>lactose-free powder</a>, is the better start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip both if you are vegan or dairy-free, for obvious reasons. Naked sells a pea protein and Optimum Nutrition a plant protein, and our <a href='/best-plant-based-protein-powder/'>best plant-based protein powder</a> guide compares those against the rest of the field.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip both if you want a flavored powder with no artificial sweeteners and a sport certification at once. Gold Standard has the certification but uses acesulfame potassium; unflavored Naked has no sweetener but no sport certification we could verify. Brands that do both exist, at a higher price; the NSF guide linked above lists them.
          </p>

          <p>
            And skip both if you want a clean-label grass-fed whey with flavor already in it. That is a different category, closer to Levels or Clean Simple Eats; our sibling comparison <a href='/clean-simple-eats-vs-levels/'>Clean Simple Eats vs Levels</a> covers two of the best-known options.
          </p>
        </div>

