        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            16. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing scoops instead of servings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Naked Whey's 30 g serving is about two scoops; Gold Standard's 31 g serving is one. Buyers who switch brands and keep using one scoop end up with about half the protein they think. Measure by the serving size in grams on the label, and scale to the protein you want.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every Gold Standard flavor is sport-certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                NSF Certified for Sport is granted flavor by flavor. When we checked, only Double Rich Chocolate and Vanilla Ice Cream were listed. If you are drug tested, a Cookies & Cream or Mocha Cappuccino tub is not covered just because it says Gold Standard on the front.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating NSF Contents Certified as a sport certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Contents Certified checks label accuracy and contaminants; it does not screen for banned substances. Naked's badge, which we could not confirm in NSF's public listing for Naked Whey, is not a substitute for NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport if your sport tests.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pricing Gold Standard by the small tub</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gold Standard is cheap only in the big tubs. At ON's store price the 1.98 lb tub worked out to about $2.68 per 25 g, against about $1.44 for the 58-serving tub at Target. Compare cost per 25 g of protein, not the sticker price.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying unflavored Naked to drink with water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Unflavored Naked Whey tastes of milk and has no lecithin to help it dissolve, so buyers often find it flat and foamy in a shaker. It shines in smoothies and recipes. If you mainly drink shakes with water, a flavored powder will be easier to stick with.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            17. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Naked vs Optimum Nutrition: which is better?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most people, Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard 100% Whey: it costs about $1.44 per 25 g of protein against about $1.84 for Naked Whey, mixes in one scoop, and two flavors are NSF Certified for Sport. Naked Whey is better if you want one ingredient, grass-fed dairy and no sweetener, mostly for smoothies and baking.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Naked Whey third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Naked says Naked Whey is NSF Contents Certified, and the badge is on its label image. When our database checked NSF's public listing for Naked's parent company, Naked Whey was not listed, so we treat it as a brand claim. It is not a sport certification and does not test for banned substances.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Optimum Nutrition Gold Standard NSF certified?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, in two flavors. Double Rich Chocolate and Vanilla Ice Cream appeared in the NSF Certified for Sport database when we checked on 19 November 2026; the other flavors did not. Certification is product-specific, so check the exact flavor and size on the NSF site before relying on it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which has more protein, Naked Whey or Gold Standard?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Naked Whey, slightly: 25 g of protein in a 30 g serving against 24 g in a 31 g scoop of Gold Standard Double Rich Chocolate, per the labels at the time of writing. That makes Naked about 83% protein by weight against about 77%. Both come to roughly 120 to 125 calories per 25 g of protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Gold Standard have artificial sweeteners?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. The Double Rich Chocolate label lists acesulfame potassium, along with natural and artificial flavor and sunflower and/or soy lecithin. Other flavors vary, so check the tub. Unflavored Naked Whey has no sweetener of any kind; its only ingredient is whey protein concentrate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Naked Whey good for lactose intolerance?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not especially. Naked Whey is 100% whey concentrate, which keeps more lactose than isolate, and it adds no lactase. Gold Standard's blend leads with isolate and shows 1 g of sugar against Naked's 2 g, so it is likely a little gentler. If lactose reliably bothers you, choose a pure whey isolate instead.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Naked Whey worth the higher price?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is if a one-ingredient, unsweetened, grass-fed label matters to you, and you mainly use protein in smoothies or recipes. The premium is about 40 cents per 25 g, or around $12 a month at one serving a day. If you just want a good-tasting, tested whey at the lowest cost, Gold Standard is the better value.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

